#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Attendance Tracker v4 - Excel workbook builder
----------------------------------------------
Builds Attendance_Tracker.xlsx with these tabs:
  Dashboard, Guide, Employee Master, Setup, Codes,
  Jan-2026 ... Dec-2027 (one tab per month) and a hidden Helper sheet.
No macros. The formulas only use functions available in Excel 2010 and later.

New in v4
  - Dashboard charts: legends sit centred below each chart (Excel was drawing them on top of
    the chart, over the month labels).

New in v3
  - All formulas are hidden: they don't show in the formula bar on any sheet
    (optional SHEET_PASSWORD setting below).
  - Month tabs: the code dropdown ends with a blank item (after WO) that clears a cell.

New in v2
  - Codes: "Counts as" is fixed text (no dropdown). RT_0 counts as
    "Leave/ISPL Holiday" and RT_SH as "Statutory Holiday".
  - Employee Master: new Vendor column (G); Status moves to H. The Setup tab
    has a Vendors list behind the Vendor dropdowns.
  - Dashboard: Vendor filter, plus a team-member table with one row per
    employee (Present, Leave/ISPL Holiday, Early Morning, Late Evening,
    Statutory Holiday, Week Off, Attendance %).
  - Month tabs: Vendor column (G); the day grid is H:AL, employee totals AM:AR.

Needs : Python 3.6+ and openpyxl 3.0 or newer (it comes with Anaconda).
        Check:    python -c "import openpyxl; print(openpyxl.__version__)"
        Install:  pip install openpyxl
Run   : python build_attendance_tracker_v4.py
        python build_attendance_tracker_v4.py D:/Reports/My_Tracker.xlsx
Then  : open the file in Excel. All formulas calculate when it opens
        (a few seconds); save it once.

Safety: the script never overwrites an existing file (add --force if you
really want that). Each run creates a NEW, EMPTY tracker - it does not
update a tracker you are already using.
"""
